  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 123 AM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nWeather pattern affecting the forecast area has not changed as\nridge continues to sit and meander across the southwestern U.S.\nHeights will continue to remain elevated and with efficient\nsouthwesterly downslope flow, we expect another day of near to \nrecord breaking heat across the forecast area. A Heat Advisory \nhas been issued CWA-wide to highlight widespread 105-110 degree \ntemperatures. It should be noted that heat indices are not the \ndriving factor for this issuance, just the actual air \ntemperature. We are allowed the discretion, despite heat indices\nnot much of a factor, to issue heat headlines after \ncollaboration with neighboring offices. It was determined given \nthe prolonged stretch of the intense heat, combined with \nconsistent messaging, that a headline was warranted. Heat \nprecautions should be taken to mitigate the risk of heat related\nillnesses. Additional heat headlines may be needed into early \nnext week, but those decisions will be made on a day to day \nbasis.\n\nSimilarly to today, isolated thunderstorms will be possible to\ndevelop. The coverage is expected to be low (10-20%), but again\nfavoring west and northwestern zones. The risks from any storm\nwould be gusty winds 50-60 mph given average sounding DCAPEs\naround 2,000 J/kg again. Another risk would be the lightning\ninduced grassland fire potential. With dew points in the 40s and\nair temperatures exceeding triple digits, rainfall reaching the\nground would be limited. Given strong potential winds in and \naround storms, if a fire were to start, it could spread rapidly.\nSPC Fire Weather forecast does outline a isolated dry \nthunderstorm risk to highlight this potential.\n\nNot much has changed in the longer term period as lower\ntemperatures and higher chance of rainfall remains evident.\nLREF grand ensemble and NBM probabilities continue to maintain\nwidespread 30-70% POPs each evening/night from Wednesday and \ninto next weekend. High temperatures return into the 80s by late\nweek.",
